Here are the complete findings, with line numbers from the full file already read above:

---

## 1. How DM channels are filtered/displayed — **NO filtering by active project**

At **line 48-49**:
```javascript
const groupChannels = channels.filter((c) => c.kind === "group");
const dmChannels = channels.filter((c) => c.kind === "dm");
```

`dmChannels` is simply `channels.filter((c) => c.kind === "dm")` — **every single DM the admin belongs to, across all projects**, is included. There is zero filtering by `rosterProjectId` or any project scope.

Then, at **lines 140-174** (the entire "Direct" section):
```jsx
<div className="p-3 border-t border-white/10">
  <p className="px-2 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 mb-1">
    Direct
  </p>
  {dmChannels.length === 0 ? (
    <p className="px-2 text-xs text-gray-600">No direct messages yet</p>
  ) : (
    dmChannels.map((channel) => {
      const other = otherDmMember(channel);
      return (
        <button ...>
          <MessageCircle className="w-4 h-4 shrink-0" />
          <PresenceDot online={other?.isOnline} />
          <span className="truncate flex-1">
            {other?.name || "Direct message"}
          </span>
          <UnreadBadge count={channel.unreadCount} />
        </button>
      );
    })
  )}
</div>
```

Every DM channel in the admin's entire universe is rendered here. There is no `.filter(dm => dm.projectId === rosterProjectId)`, so DMs from unrelated projects leak into the sidebar.

---

## 2. How the DM label (other person's name) is resolved

At **lines 52-57**, inside the `ChannelSidebar` function body, but *outside* the render loop:
```javascript
const otherDmMember = (channel) => {
  const otherUserId = (channel.memberUserIds || []).find(
    (id) => id !== currentUserId,
  );
  return members.find((m) => m.userId === otherUserId) || null;
};
```

It:

1. Takes `channel.memberUserIds` (e.g. `["adminId", "clientId"]`).
2. Finds the one that is **not** `currentUserId` → that's the DM partner.
3. Looks up that `userId` in the `members` roster.

At **line 167**, the label fallback is:
```javascript
{other?.name || "Direct message"}
```

If `members` doesn't contain the partner (because the DM belongs to a *different* project whose members weren't fetched), `other` is `null`, and it renders the generic **"Direct message"** string.

---

## 3. Where `members` comes from

At **lines 50-51**:
```javascript
const activeChannel = channels.find((c) => c._id === activeChannelId);
const rosterProjectId =
  activeChannel?.projectId || groupChannels[0]?.projectId || null;
const { members } = useProjectMembers(rosterProjectId);
```

`rosterProjectId` is determined by:
- The **currently active channel's** `projectId` (if one is selected), OR
- The **first group channel's** `projectId` (fallback).

Then `useProjectMembers(rosterProjectId)` calls `GET /api/client-projects/${projectId}/members` — fetching members for **only one project**. The hook is defined in `hooks/useProjectMembers.js` at lines 18-28:

```javascript
const membersQuery = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['project-members', projectId],
  enabled: !!projectId,
  queryFn: async () => {
    const res = await axios.get(
      `/api/client-projects/${projectId}/members`,
      { headers: getAuthHeaders() },
    );
    return res.data; // { members, invitations }
  },
});
```

So `members` is always scoped to a single project.

---

## Root Cause Summary

The bug has **two halves** that combine:

| # | Problem | Location |
|---|---------|----------|
| **1** | `dmChannels` is **not filtered** by `rosterProjectId`. Admin sees DMs from *all* projects. | Line 49: `const dmChannels = channels.filter((c) => c.kind === "dm");` |
| **2** | `members` is fetched for **only one project** (`rosterProjectId`). DMs from other projects can't resolve the partner's name. | Line 51: `useProjectMembers(rosterProjectId)` |

The fix should filter `dmChannels` to only DMs whose `projectId` matches `rosterProjectId`. That means changing line 49 to something like:

```javascript
const dmChannels = channels.filter(
  (c) => c.kind === "dm" && (!rosterProjectId || c.projectId === rosterProjectId)
);
```

(With the `!rosterProjectId` guard so it doesn't hide everything before any project is selected — though the sidebar also needs `rosterProjectId` to resolve names, so this is a tight coupling.)

The `channels` array itself comes from `useChatChannels()` (in `hooks/useProjectChat.js`, line 14-20), which calls `GET /api/chat/channels`. That endpoint returns every channel the user belongs to — the server does not scope by project, so the responsibility for project-scoped filtering falls entirely on the client-side code in `ChannelSidebar.jsx`.

